<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Membrane Hub hardened-runtime entitlements — rationale document, not
  currently wired into the build.

  RightKit's canonical macOS pipeline (`pnpm release:build:mac`, see
  docs/release/macos.md) builds via `tauri build`, which reads entitlements
  from apps/membrane-hub/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json#bundle.macOS.entitlements.
  That key is unset today, so Tauri applies no custom entitlements file —
  behaviorally identical to this file's empty <dict/>, since it grants no
  exception either. apps/membrane-hub/** is owned by a concurrent lane and is
  out of scope for this task, so this file is not referenced from
  tauri.conf.json yet. It is kept as the recorded rationale for why no
  hardened-runtime exception is needed, and as the file to wire in (via
  bundle.macOS.entitlements) the day one actually is.

  No hardened-runtime exception entitlement is granted. Rationale:
  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation is not needed because
    every embedded binary (crypt-service daemon, membrane CLI/MCP) is signed
    under the same Developer ID team as the Hub app.
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory are
    not needed because the Hub renders with WKWebView, an Apple-signed,
    out-of-process WebContent process, not an in-process JS engine.
  - No App Sandbox network entitlement is declared because sidecar<->Hub
    communication stays on loopback/stdio, matching the Membrane invariant
    "keep data local, loopback-bound, and repository-confined"
    (membrane/docs/agent-rules.md).

  Add an exception entitlement here, and wire this file into
  tauri.conf.json#bundle.macOS.entitlements, only when a concrete,
  reproducible hardened-runtime failure requires it, and record the reason
  in docs/release/macos.md next to it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
